---
// Breweries — the thirteenth and last of the "by type of premises" leaf pages
// under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of premises"
// group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AT ALL, given that "Breweries" looks like the
// oddest entry in a thirteen-item menu: the Northern Rivers and the Tweed
// have an unusually dense craft brewing scene for their population, and a
// brewery is genuinely its own premises type rather than a factory with a
// bar attached. It is the only entry in the cluster that is a PRODUCTION
// SITE and a HOSPITALITY VENUE in one building, on one lease, cleaned by one
// contractor, to two completely different standards on either side of a
// glass wall. That is the page.
//
// ⚠️ THE ONE LINE NOBODY MAY CROSS, and it is the whole editorial spine:
// CIP — clean in place, the cleaning of the inside of tanks, lines and
// vessels — IS THE BREWERS' JOB AND ONLY THE BREWERS' JOB. It is part of
// making beer, it uses caustic and acid at concentrations that will injure
// someone untrained, it happens in and around confined spaces, and it is the
// step that determines whether a batch is infected. A cleaning contractor
// who offers it is offering to ruin beer and hurt people. §3 refuses it in
// the first substantive block and the refusal is repeated in the scope and
// in two FAQs, because this is the one thing on the page a keen salesperson
// might try to soften.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT structurally:
//   - `Hero variant="split-collage"` — THE ONLY USE OF THAT VARIANT IN THE
//     CLUSTER, and it earns it: the page's argument is that this is two
//     buildings in one, so the hero shows both sides at once rather than
//     picking a side.
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on CIP, first.
//   - `ServiceBlocks variant="highlight"` for the two zones plus the
//     boundary — the second use of `highlight` after the medical page, and
//     for the same reason: the reader picks the side of the wall they are
//     asking about.
//   - `ContentGrid columns={2}` on drains, which sounds like a small subject
//     and is the single most important surface in a brewery.
//   - No `ComparisonTable`, no `MetricsBlock`.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - Does TLB do breweries today? Nothing in the brand foundation document
//     says so. Along with factories, this is the most speculative of the
//     thirteen.
//   - Whether TLB will work on a brew deck AT ALL while the brewery is
//     operating, given wet floors, hot liquor, CO2 in cellar spaces and
//     caustic in use nearby. Bracketed in §3. If the answer is no, this page
//     honestly covers the taproom and the amenities only, and §4's first
//     panel comes off.
//   - CO2 AND OXYGEN DEPLETION. Fermentation and cellar spaces can hold CO2
//     at levels that displace oxygen with no warning to the person walking
//     in. Whether TLB's team is briefed on this and whether sites provide
//     monitoring is bracketed in §3, and it is the most serious safety
//     bracket on the page.
//   - Spent grain and trade waste: whether TLB touches either. Bracketed in
//     §4. Both have their own arrangements and neither is a cleaning job.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import ImageBand from '../../components/sections/ImageBand.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// ⚠️ UNIFORM NOTE. The two production-side frames use the canonical aqua
// polo and dark teal apron from IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 but swap shoes for tall
// rubber gumboots, because a brew deck floor is wet by design and a frame of
// someone standing in running water in sneakers would be a photo of a
// hazard. No hi-vis vest here, unlike the construction, warehouse and
// factory pages: a small brewery production space is not a vehicle traffic
// zone, and adding a vest would misrepresent the setting.
//
// ⚠️ NO BEER BRANDING ANYWHERE, and this is stricter than the sitewide "no
// text" rule. The taproom frame's tap bank is deliberately unbadged and the
// tanks are unlabelled. A generated brewery photo with invented brand marks
// on the taps would be a fabricated brand asset (IMAGE-GUIDELINES §4) AND
// would read as a real client's premises, which §7 forbids.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { nswLocationGroups } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. "Brewery cleaning" is the head term. The description names the
// CIP boundary, because a brewer searching this phrase is often looking for
// exactly the thing this page refuses, and they should be able to tell from
// the search result rather than after a phone call.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §1 Hero. `split-collage` — THE ONLY USE OF THIS VARIANT IN THE CLUSTER.
//
// It earns it here and nowhere else: this page's entire argument is that a
// brewery is two buildings on one lease, so the hero has to show both sides
// of the glass wall at once. Every other premises page has one subject and a
// single frame is the right answer; picking one frame here would pick a side
// and lose half the reader's recognition.
//
// `collageImages` flows alternately into two stacked columns (index 0 left,
// 1 right, 2 left), so the order below is: brew deck, taproom, keg line.
// Production, hospitality, production — which is the page in miniature.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. Same standing note as every premises page,
// plus the uniform and no-branding notes in the imports above.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block. Paragraph two draws the CIP line in the
// reader's own vocabulary before §3 refuses it formally, because a brewer
// who reaches §3 without this paragraph will read the refusal as ignorance
// rather than as discipline.

// §3 CIP and CO2. `warning`, and FIRST — before any scope.
//
// THE SPINE OF THE PAGE. See the file header: this is the one block a keen
// salesperson might try to soften, and it must not be softened.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and this block is as serious as the
// construction page's silica callout:
//   - CIP uses caustic (typically sodium hydroxide) and acid cycles at
//     concentrations and temperatures that cause serious chemical burns and
//     eye injury. Correct.
//   - Tank interiors are confined spaces; entry requires a permit,
//     atmospheric testing and specific training. Correct.
//   - Fermentation produces CO2 in large volumes; CO2 is heavier than air,
//     collects at low level and in cellars and tank pits, and displaces
//     oxygen with essentially no warning to the person entering. Correct,
//     and it is a documented cause of death in breweries and wineries.
//     NO PPM FIGURE IS QUOTED, on purpose — exposure standards move and a
//     wrong number in a safety warning is worse than none.
//   - Whether monitoring is present is a site question, which is why the
//     paragraph asks rather than asserts.
//   - SafeWork NSW and Safe Work Australia are the right primary sources and
//     the brand foundation document §3.3 already approves them.

// §4 The two sides plus the boundary. `ServiceBlocks variant="highlight"` —
// the second use of that variant in the cluster after the medical page, and
// for the same reason: the reader picks rather than reads through. A brewer
// wants the production panel, a venue manager wants the taproom panel, and
// the owner of a small brewery wants both plus the boundary spelled out.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Panel one carries the brew-deck access bracket and panel three the spent
// grain and trade waste brackets.
//
// In `zoneItems`, on "Production side: floors, drains and tank surrounds" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Whether TLB works this side at all decides how much
// of this page is honest.
// In `zoneItems`, on "The boundary: what stays with you" (description):
// ⚠️ Two real brackets. Neither spent grain nor trade waste is a
// cleaning job, but both get asked for.

// §5 Drains. `ContentGrid columns={2}` with `feature` cells.
//
// This looks like a small subject and it is the most important surface in the
// building, which is exactly why it gets its own section rather than a line
// in the scope. Every brewer already knows this; every venue manager and
// every landlord does not.

// §6 "Worth knowing". The lever here is unique in the cluster: on every other
// premises type poor cleaning costs money slowly. In a brewery it can cost a
// whole batch, and a brewer can price that themselves.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED:
//   - Beer spoilage organisms of concern include wild yeast (notably
//     Brettanomyces) and lactic acid bacteria (Lactobacillus, Pediococcus).
//     Correct.
//   - Drains, floors and hose ends are recognised environmental reservoirs
//     for these organisms in breweries. Correct in substance.
//   - Beer is a food for regulatory purposes in Australia and a brewery is a
//     food business, so premises cleanliness obligations under the Food
//     Standards Code apply. Correct in substance; no clause quoted.
//   - A taproom serving food has the same obligations as any other food
//     service premises. Correct.
//   - NO SPOILAGE STATISTIC OR DOLLAR FIGURE IS QUOTED, because any specific
//     number would need a source this page cannot cite, and the paragraph
//     asks the reader to do their own sum instead.

// §7 Related pages. Hospitality and commercial kitchen are the two nearest
// neighbours — a taproom is a venue and most of them now serve food — and
// factories is the production-side neighbour.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/brewery-cleaning/');

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — a brewer, owner or taproom
// manager.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Will you do our CIP?" (answer):
// ⚠️ The page's spine. This answer must not be softened, and it is the
// reason the same refusal appears in §2, §3, §4 and twice in the FAQs.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Can you work while we are brewing?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Real bracket, and it decides how much of the production side TLB
// can actually offer.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Are you across the CO2 risk?" (answer):
// ⚠️ The most serious safety bracket on the page.

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. Checkable claims:
//   - CIP definition and its caustic/acid cycles. Correct.
//   - Tank interiors are confined spaces requiring permit and testing.
//     Correct.
//   - CO2 is heavier than air, pools at low level, displaces oxygen, and has
//     caused deaths in breweries and wineries. Correct; no ppm figure quoted.
//   - Beer spoilage organisms: wild yeast including Brettanomyces, and lactic
//     acid bacteria including Lactobacillus and Pediococcus. Correct.
//   - Beer is a food and a brewery a food business under the Food Standards
//     Code; premises cleanliness obligations apply. Correct in substance; no
//     clause quoted.
//   - Trade waste from a brewery is typically regulated by the local water
//     authority under a trade waste agreement. Correct in substance.
//   - Spent grain is usually collected as stockfeed under its own
//     arrangement. Correct in substance.
//   - No dollar figure anywhere, same as every sibling page.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does brewery cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.

// §11 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/brewery-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Brewery cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/brewery-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.heroCollage,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    cip: section.callout,
    // A panel with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    zones: section.imageCards,
    drainPhoto: section.imageBand,
    drains: section.contentGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings.
    related: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The towns come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/brewery-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const collageImages = page.hero.collageImages.map((image) => sized(image, '4/3'));

const zoneItems = page.zones.items.map((item) => ({
  ...item,
  image: item.image && sized(item.image, '4/3'),
  cta: item.cta ?? walkthroughCta,
}));

const drainBlocks = page.drains.items.map((item) =>
  item.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(item.image, '4/3') } : item,
);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    {/* §1 — the only `split-collage` hero in the cluster. Three frames,
        production / hospitality / production, because the page's argument is
        that this is two buildings on one lease and a single hero image would
        have to pick a side. */}
    <Hero
      variant="split-collage"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      collageImages={collageImages}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — the spine of the page, before any scope. Not to be softened; see
        the file header. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.cip.heading} body={page.cip.body} cta={page.cip.cta} />

    {/* §4 — the second `highlight` in the cluster after the medical page. The
        reader picks the side of the wall they are asking about rather than
        reading through all three. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="highlight"
      heading={page.zones.heading}
      lead={page.zones.lead}
      note={page.zones.note}
      tileCtaLabel={page.zones.tileCtaLabel}
      items={zoneItems}
    />

    {/* The drain frame, given a band of its own because §5 is about to argue
        that this unglamorous strip of stainless is the most important surface
        in the building, and the photo makes that legible before the argument
        starts.

        ⚠️ Source is 4:3 in a 16/9 box, so `cover` crops top and bottom.
        Checked: the trench drain, the lifted grate end and the kegs all
        survive the crop, which is everything the caption claims. */}
    <ImageBand
      ratio="16/9"
      label={page.drainPhoto.image.label}
      caption={page.drainPhoto.caption}
      src={page.drainPhoto.image.src}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.drains.heading} lead={page.drains.lead} columns={2} blocks={drainBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading}
      groups={nswLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
